<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Equatorial Guinea</h1><p class="st-meta">1968 CE – present · capital Malabo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small state on Africa's west coast and its only Spanish-speaking country, independent in 1968, under Obiang since 1979, an oil producer since the 1990s.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-guinea">Spanish Guinea</a> gained independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on 12 October 1968 as the Republic of Equatorial Guinea, after a referendum in 1963 had given it autonomy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> had pressed Spain. Francisco Macías Nguema became president in the country's only free and fair election to date.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Macías outlawed opposition parties in July 1970, made himself president for life in 1972 and broke off ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the West. In 1974 the World Council of Churches said large numbers of people had been murdered, and an estimated 80,000 were killed out of a population of 300,000. His nephew Teodoro Obiang deposed him on 3 August 1979 in a coup and succeeded him. Mobil discovered oil in 1995, and the country developed rapidly, though the earnings from its oil have not reached the population.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>A new constitution was approved in November 2011, limiting the president to two seven-year terms. Obiang was reelected in 2016, and after the 2022 elections his party holds all 100 seats in the Chamber of Deputies. In 2022 he was re-elected with 99.7% of the vote amid accusations of fraud by the opposition. The capital has been Ciudad de la Paz since January 2026.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/equatorial-guinea.jpg" alt="Equatorial Guinea" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Equatorial_Guinea.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1968 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Malabo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Francisco Macías Nguema, Teodoro Obiang</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity (mainly Roman Catholic), Islam, animism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish, French, Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Equatorial Guinea</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Equatorial Guinea</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1997&amp;focus=equatorial-guinea" aria-label="Equatorial Guinea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4958 2121 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Equatorial Guinea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5278 2377L5287 2374"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5287 2374h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5278 2377h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5278 2377h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1997&amp;focus=equatorial-guinea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
